<?xml version='1.0' encoding='utf-8' standalone='no'?>
<!DOCTYPE issue SYSTEM "lwg-issue.dtd">

<issue num="4638" status="New">
<title>`std::abs(float)` is underspecified</title>
<section>
<sref ref="[c.math.abs]"/>
</section>
<submitter>Jan Schultke</submitter>
<date>06 Nov 2026</date>
<priority>99</priority>

<discussion>
<p>
<sref ref="[c.math.abs]"/> states that <tt>std::abs(<i>floating-point-type</i> x)</tt>:
</p>
<blockquote style="border-left: 3px solid #ccc;padding-left: 15px;">
<p>
<i>Returns</i>: The absolute value of `x`.
</p>
</blockquote>
<p>
What is the absolute value of NaN? What happens when passing signaling NaN? What about infinity? 
Does it produce positive zero when given negative zero? What about floating-point exceptions? 
When is it a constant expression? Nobody knows.
<p/>
In practice, all major standard libraries forward to `std::fabs`.
</p>
</discussion>

<resolution>
<p>
This wording is relative to <paper num="N5054"/>.
</p>
<ol>
<li>
<p>Modify <sref ref="[c.math.abs]"/> as indicated:</p>

<blockquote>
<pre>
constexpr floating-point-type abs(<i>floating-point-type</i> x);
</pre>
<blockquote>
<p>
-4- <i><del>Returns</del><ins>Effects</ins></i>: <del>The absolute value of `x`</del><ins> Equivalent to: `return fabs(x);`</ins>.
</p>
</blockquote>
<p>
<span style="font-variant: small-caps">See also:</span> ISO/IEC 9899:2024, 7.12.8.3, 7.24.7.1
</p>
</blockquote>

</li>

</ol>
</resolution>

</issue>
